 "label": "Going out for fun",
 "topic": "Hobbies, sport & games",
 "rationale": "Everyday plans to go out and do something, all built the same way: the activity noun goes straight before ni iku.",
 "mixUps": "English 'go for a walk' or 'go to karaoke' tempts a verb before ni iku, but the noun goes straight in: sanpo ni iku, karaoke ni iku. eiga ni iku is the outing and eiga o miru the watching. asobi ni iku also means dropping by someone's place (asobi ni kite ne).",
 "targets": [
  {
   "id": "c-asobi-ni-iku",
   "romaji": "asobi ni iku",
   "polite": "asobi ni ikimasu",
   "english": "go out for fun; go visit",
   "ja": "遊びに行く",
   "kana": "あそびにいく",
   "politeJa": "遊びに行きます",
   "noun": "asobi",
   "particle": "ni",
   "verb": "iku",
   "verbJa": "行く",
   "verbSense": "go (asobi is the noun of asobu, have fun, hang out)",
   "englishVerb": "go out",
   "trap": "English 'play' makes asobu sound childish, but adults say asobi ni iku all the time for a fun outing or a visit to a friend's place. And 'I'll come and visit' is asobi ni iku, not asobi ni kuru: as with mukae ni iku, you are the one going.",
   "partners": [
    "nomi (drinks: nomi ni iku)",
    "omimai (visiting someone sick: omimai ni iku)",
    "aisatsu (dropping by to say hello: aisatsu ni iku)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Covers a fun day or night out and a visit to someone's home: shuumatsu shibuya ni asobi ni itta, tomodachi no uchi ni asobi ni iku. Casual speech often drops ni (asobi iku?). The mirror image, kondo asobi ni kite, is the standard 'come over sometime'.",
   "notes": "Among friends kondo asobou (let's hang out sometime) is the plain invitation, often a friendly maybe rather than a fixed plan.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "kondo, asobi ni iku ne!",
    "ja": "今度、遊びに行くね！",
    "english": "I'll come and visit sometime!"
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "kondo, asobi ni ikimasu ne!",
    "ja": "今度、遊びに行きますね！",
    "english": "I'll come and visit sometime!"
   }
  },
